  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safeguarding African Grey Parrot populations in the Republic of Congo through a coordinated national and transboundary response to combat illegal trafficking</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">African grey parrots are intensively hunted throughout their range for the trade in live birds and parrot parts. Since the 1990s, much of the legal trade has shifted from West Africa to Central Africa where local populations are rapidly declining. African grey parrots were uplisted to Appendix I at the 17th CITES Conference of the Parties (CoP) in 2016. This uplisting bans the commercial trade and harvest of wild-caught parrots. In the Republic of Congo (ROC), wild parrots are frequently seized from traffickers, including those crossing the border from Cameroon and Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) to illegally harvest parrots. To address the illegal trapping and killing of wild parrots for the live bird and parts trade, this two-year grant will support: collection and standardization of information on parrot trafficking routes to better target law enforcement operations; law enforcement operations to interdict parrot trafficking; training for prosecutors and magistrates on national and international (CITES) legislation to strengthen the application of wildlife laws; technical and legal support to reform national CITES implementation legislation, including support at the next CITES CoP in 2019; and bilateral discussions between ROC and DRC authorities to help combat transboundary parrot trafficking.</narrative>
  </description>
